<urn:anchor:167> <urn:quote> "Briefly, a linear space is a set of elements of any kind on which certain operations (called addition and multiplication by numbers) can be performed." .
<urn:anchor:168> <urn:quote> "If we specify the set V and tell how to add its elements and how to multiply them by numbers, we get a concrete example of a linear space." .
<urn:anchor:169> <urn:quote> "In defining a linear space, we do not specify the nature of the elements nor do we tell how the operations are to be performed on them." .
<urn:anchor:16> <urn:quote> "If $x$ and $y$ are elements in a Euclidean space, with $y \\neq O$ , the element" .
<urn:anchor:170> <urn:quote> "Let V denote a nonempty set of objects, called elements. The set V is called a linear space if it satisfies the following ten axioms which we list in three groups." .
<urn:anchor:171> <urn:quote> "Instead, we require that the operations have certain properties which we take as axioms for a linear space." .
<urn:anchor:172> <urn:quote> "Let V denote a nonempty set of objects, called elements. The set V is called a linear space if it satisfies the following ten axioms which we list in three groups." .
<urn:anchor:173> <urn:quote> "We turn now to a detailed description of these axioms." .
<urn:anchor:174> <urn:quote> "When a theorem is deduced from the axioms of a linear space, we obtain, in one stroke, a result valid for each concrete example." .
<urn:anchor:175> <urn:quote> "These examples and many others illustrate how the linear space concept permeates algebra, geometry, and analysis." .
<urn:anchor:176> <urn:quote> "Sometimes a linear space is referred to as a linear vector space or simply a vector space; the numbers used as multipliers are also called scalars." .
<urn:anchor:177> <urn:quote> "When we use the term linear space without further designation, it is to be understood that the space can be real or complex." .
<urn:anchor:178> <urn:quote> "Although we shall deal primarily with examples of real linear spaces, all the theorems are valid for complex linear spaces as well." .
<urn:anchor:179> <urn:quote> "First of all, the real numbers themselves are such objects. Other examples are real-valued functions, the complex numbers, infinite series, vectors in n-space, and vector-valued functions." .
<urn:anchor:17> <urn:quote> "is called the projection of x along y." .
<urn:anchor:180> <urn:quote> "we discuss a general mathematical concept, called a linear space, which includes all these examples and many others as special cases." .
<urn:anchor:181> <urn:quote> "AXIOM 1. CLOSURE UNDER ADDITION. For every pair of elements x and y in V there corresponds a unique element in V called the sum of x and y, denoted by $x + y$ ." .
<urn:anchor:182> <urn:quote> "AXIOM 2. CLOSURE UNDER MULTIPLICATION BY REAL NUMBERS. For every x in V and every real number a there corresponds an element in V called the product of a and x, denoted by ax." .
