<urn:anchor:648> <urn:quote> "Briefly, a linear space is a set of elements of any kind on which certain operations (called addition and multiplication by numbers) can be performed." .
<urn:anchor:649> <urn:quote> "The set V is called a linear space if it satisfies the following ten axioms which we list in three groups." .
<urn:anchor:64> <urn:quote> "In case $(x, y)$ is not an inner product, tell which axioms are not satisfied." .
<urn:anchor:650> <urn:quote> "In this chapter we discuss a general mathematical concept, called a linear space, which includes all these examples and many others as special cases." .
<urn:anchor:651> <urn:quote> "AXIOM 1. CLOSURE UNDER ADDITION. For every pair of elements x and y in V there corresponds a unique element in V called the sum of x and y" .
<urn:anchor:652> <urn:quote> "AXIOM 2. CLOSURE UNDER MULTIPLICATION BY REAL NUMBERS. For every x in V and every real number a there corresponds an element in V called the product of a and x, denoted by ax." .
<urn:anchor:653> <urn:quote> "AXIOM 3. COMMUTATIVE LAW. For all x and y in V, we have $x + y = y + x$" .
<urn:anchor:654> <urn:quote> "AXIOM 4. ASSOCIATIVE LAW. For all $x, y$ , and $z$ in $V$ , we have $(x + y) + z = x + (y + z)$" .
<urn:anchor:655> <urn:quote> "AXIOM 6. EXISTENCE OF NEGATIVES. For every x in V, the element $(-1)x$ has the property" .
<urn:anchor:656> <urn:quote> "Axioms for multiplication by numbers" .
<urn:anchor:657> <urn:quote> "AXIOM 7. ASSOCIATIVE LAW. For every x in V and all real numbers a and b, we have" .
<urn:anchor:658> <urn:quote> "AXIOM 10. EXISTENCE OF IDENTITY. For every x in V, we have 1x = x." .
<urn:anchor:659> <urn:quote> "Sometimes a linear space is referred to as a linear vector space or simply a vector space; the numbers used as multipliers are also called scalars." .
<urn:anchor:65> <urn:quote> "In case $(f,g)$ is not an inner product, indicate which axioms are violated." .
<urn:anchor:660> <urn:quote> "A real linear space has real numbers as scalars; a complex linear space has complex numbers as scalars." .
<urn:anchor:661> <urn:quote> "Linear spaces, as defined above, are sometimes called real linear spaces to emphasize the fact that we are multiplying the elements of V by real numbers." .
<urn:anchor:662> <urn:quote> "If real number is replaced by complex number in Axioms 2, 7, 8, and 9, the resulting structure is called a complex linear space." .
<urn:anchor:663> <urn:quote> "Although we shall deal primarily with examples of real linear spaces, all the theorems are valid for complex linear spaces as well." .
